## The Value Attribute vs. The Display Label

When you submit a form containing a `<select>` element, the data sent to your server is determined strictly by the `value` attribute of the selected option tag. In your specific case, the HTML defines three distinct pieces of information: the label ("Toyota"), the visual text shown in the browser, and the underlying value (`2`).

```html
<option value="2">Toyota</option>
```

Your current PHP code executes `$_POST['Make']`, which correctly retrieves the string "2" because that is what the browser sends upon submission. The discrepancy arises from a common misconception: developers often assume that whatever they see on the screen is what gets sent to the server. To display "Toyota" instead of "2", you need to alter how the form submits its data, or change how the options are generated.

## Retrieving Display Text Without Modifying HTML

If you cannot change the HTML structureâperhaps because it is part of a legacy system or managed by another teamâyou must rely on client-side logic to bridge the gap before submission. The most robust approach involves using JavaScript to intercept the form submission. By attaching an event listener to the submit button, you can dynamically fetch the `text` property of the selected `<option>` element and replace the default value in the input field or send a custom payload.

Here is a practical implementation pattern:

```javascript
document.querySelector('form').addEventListener('submit', function(e) {
    const select = document.getElementById('cmbMake');
    const selectedText = select.options[select.selectedIndex].text;

    // Prevent default submission to handle the data differently,
    // or modify the POST variable directly if using AJAX.
    e.preventDefault(); 

    console.log('Visual Text:', selectedText); 
    // In a real scenario, you might send this value via an AJAX request
    // rather than letting the browser submit the form with value="2".
});
```

However, if you must use standard HTTP POST and cannot alter the `name` attribute or add hidden fields, modifying the HTML is the cleaner architectural choice.

## Modifying the Form Structure for Semantic Data

The most maintainable solution is to update the `<option>` tags so that the `value` attribute matches the manufacturer name you wish to display. This aligns the data contract between the client and the server without requiring complex JavaScript workarounds.

```html
<select id="cmbMake" name="Make">
   <option value="" selected>Select Manufacturer</option>
   <option value="--Any--">--Any--</option>
   <option value="Toyota">Toyota</option>
   <option value="Nissan">Nissan</option>
</select>
```

With this adjustment, when a user selects "Toyota", the browser sends `Make=Toyota` to your PHP script. Your existing logic remains valid, but you must address a critical security and functionality issue present in the provided source material.

## Addressing Deprecated Security Practices

The PHP snippet provided utilizes `mysql_real_escape_string`, which is part of a deprecated extension removed in modern PHP versions (8.1.0). Furthermore, relying on string escaping for SQL injection prevention without using prepared statements is an outdated pattern that introduces significant risk.

Since you are retrieving manufacturer names from a database to populate the dropdown, the data should never be hardcoded or passed directly from user input without validation. The correct workflow involves querying your database:

```php
// Pseudo-code for secure implementation
$makers = DB::table('vehicles')->distinct()->pluck('manufacturer')->toArray();

foreach ($makers as $make) {
    echo '<option value="' . e($make) . '">' . e($make) . '</option>';
}
```

In this approach, the `value` and the display text are identical. When the form submits, `$_POST['Make']` will contain "Toyota", and you can use it directly in your query or logic without needing to map an integer ID back to a string name. This eliminates the need for a lookup table to translate "2" into "Toyota".

## Final Recommendation

To achieve your goal of displaying "Toyota" upon search, the most straightforward fix is ensuring that the `value` attribute of the selected option contains the manufacturer's name. While JavaScript can extract the text label from the current selection, it adds complexity and potential for errors across different browsers. Aligning the value with the label simplifies the server-side logic and ensures data integrity. Additionally, ensure you migrate away from `mysql_real_escape_string` to PDO or MySQLi prepared statements to secure your application against injection attacks.
